{"title":"1973 AHSME Problems/Problem 31","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1973_AHSME_Problems/Problem_31&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the following equation, each of the letters represents uniquely a different digit in base ten: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/7/ef7381f43a4c3a8ca116bbede93144775e68e996.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(YE) \\cdot (ME) = TTT\\]\" width=\"166\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/2/7623c7d456f3824b72ee8a4d8b82b3611b41f2de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E+M+T+Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"128\" height=\"14\" /> equals \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/e/a2e5ced0bb597ae8ae92184fc48b6609bb6345f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 19 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\ 20 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 21 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 22 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"397\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1973_AHSME_Problems/Problem_31&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The right side of the equation can be rewritten as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/f/c2f73ce7a05c116aa4bfd40ad624a5671ad41eb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$111T = 37 \\cdot 3T$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"118\" height=\"13\" />.  With trial and error and prime factorization as a guide, we can test different digits of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to see if we can find two two-digit numbers that have the same units digit and multiply to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/0/450a4b1095f06e31ee4cfe0183f89e1c4ca137f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$111T$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>The only possibility that works is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/a/70a7dc8847808d89c1da851e9900a4be3ff9620c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$37 \\cdot 27 = 999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" />.  That means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/c/76c5a8bbfde2239fdc3675b149a961a866112b7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E+M+T+Y = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 21}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"216\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1973_AHSME_Problems/Problem_31&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1973_AHSME\" title=\"1973 AHSME\">1973 AHSME</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1973_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1973 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1973_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1973 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=44&amp;year=1973\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;b